WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.647
Here is the PostgreSQL-native approach to common temporal tasks.

00:00:03.647 --> 00:00:06.412
Subtract timestamps to obtain an interval.

00:00:06.412 --> 00:00:08.917
Add INTERVAL values to shift time.

00:00:08.917 --> 00:00:11.092
Use EXTRACT to read components.

00:00:11.092 --> 00:00:15.857
Use DATE_TRUNC to change analytical grain.

00:00:15.857 --> 00:00:18.457
Use AGE when you want a symbolic calendar interval.

00:00:18.457 --> 00:00:26.632
These patterns are clearer and more portable within PostgreSQL than borrowing function names from another SQL

00:00:26.632 --> 00:00:30.812
dialect. When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:30.812 --> 00:00:35.972
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:35.972 --> 00:00:40.487
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:40.487 --> 00:00:43.607
In a real setting such as an operations team aligning timestamps across locations,

00:00:43.607 --> 00:00:50.632
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:50.632 --> 00:00:54.252
before discussing syntax.

00:00:54.252 --> 00:01:00.080
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
